The CFF subsetter runs each glyph through the subrs it calls.

Subsetting Source Han Serif, the face a CJK signature reason draws
in, failed for 127 of 128 batches of its glyphs ("Reserved
charstring byte c=0x0", "Insufficient operators on the stack").
scan_charstrings scanned each glyph and each subr apart, each on an
empty stack: operands a subr left for its caller were lost, so the
stem count, and with it the length of a hintmask, came out wrong,
and mask bytes were read as operators. It also took a glyph's font
dict from the next kept glyph and scanned local subrs in the wrong
font dict. Each kept glyph now runs from a clean state in its own
font dict, entering every subr it calls on the same stack and stem
count, ten levels deep at most. Alongside: operands left at the
first hintmask count as vstems whether or not an hstem came first;
shortint is signed and may end a subr; mul pops its operand.

